{"timestamp":"2026-05-06T17:24:01.107Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"exec_command_end","call_id":"call_kyQHsWHMlF984gVLM2AvSpx8","process_id":"51483","turn_id":"019dfe50-ef95-76f0-8722-4009764d7e19","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,220p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-29-review-checkpoint-context-design.md"],"cwd":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/entire-review","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,220p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-29-review-checkpoint-context-design.md","name":"2026-04-29-review-checkpoint-context-design.md","path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-29-review-checkpoint-context-design.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Reviewer-agent checkpoint context for `entire review`\n\n**Date:** 2026-04-29\n**Status:** Draft — locally reviewed; pending user review\n**Builds on:** PR #993 (`entire review` baseline), PR #1018 (multi-agent parallel review)\n\n## Problem\n\nToday, the agent that runs configured review skills under `entire review` is launched with a prompt that contains only:\n\n1. The list of skills to run.\n2. (PR #1018) A scope clause pinning the agent to \"commits unique to this branch vs the closest ancestor branch.\"\n3. (PR #1018) An optional per-run prompt textarea typed by the user.\n\nThe reviewer is given the artifact (the diff, the working tree, the commit graph) but **none of the per-checkpoint context that Entire already captures**: user prompts, AI-condensed summaries (intent / outcome / friction / open items), full transcripts, and per-checkpoint files-touched lists — all of which sit in committed checkpoint metadata (`entire/checkpoints/v1` today, with v2 resolution already handled by the checkpoint read path).\n\nThis is the data a human reviewer asks for in a PR comment (\"why did you do it this way?\"). The reviewer agent is being asked to evaluate the work without the story behind it, when that story is one git ref away.\n\n## Goal\n\nGive the reviewer agent enough information at launch to understand which commits it is reviewing and where to look for author-stated intent on each one — without bloating the prompt with full transcripts, without pre-curating the checkpoint data, and without introducing a new state file or commit trailer.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Pre-rendering full transcripts, prompts, or AI summaries into the prompt (token-cost cliff on long branches; the agent should pay for that selectively).\n- Surfacing rolled-back / rewound checkpoints in v1. Scope is reachable, committed work — what the author chose to ship.\n- Surfacing temporary checkpoints from shadow branches (`entire/<commit-hash>-<worktreeHash>`). Different staleness rules; different feature.\n- A new `entire context` (or similar) top-level command. Considered and dropped — see \"Rejected approaches.\"\n- Changes to checkpoint storage, commit trailers, marker handshake, condensation pipeline, or `HasReview` semantics.\n- Updating the configured review skills themselves to be checkpoint-aware. The brief is delivered via the prompt; skill code stays unchanged.\n\n## Design\n\n### Summary\n\nCompute a per-commit briefing block at `entire review` launch time and embed it into the composed prompt the agent receives. For each commit in `<base-ref>..HEAD`:\n\n- Show commit short-hash, file count, agent name (when known), subject.\n- Show the `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer ID when present, `—` otherwise.\n\nFor commits with a resolvable checkpoint, the file count comes from `CheckpointSummary.FilesTouched` and the agent comes from the latest non-review session's `CommittedMetadata.Agent`, mapped through `agent.GetByAgentType` to the registry name when known. This avoids attributing already-reviewed checkpoints to the review agent itself when a checkpoint contains both implementation and `agent_review` sessions. For commits without a checkpoint, with an unresolved checkpoint, or with only review sessions, the file count falls back to the git commit's changed-file count and the agent renders as `—`.\n\nAppend a short instruction telling the agent how to drill into per-commit context: `entire explain <id>` for the structured summary, `entire explain <id> --raw-transcript` for the full JSONL, `git show <commit>` for trailer-less commits.\n\nThe agent sees the scope upfront (no Bash discovery round-trip) and decides selectively which commits warrant a deeper read (no token-cost cliff from pre-rendered transcripts).\n\n### Prompt section ordering\n\nAfter this design lands, the composed review prompt has four sections in this order:\n\n1. Skills list — *PR #993*.\n2. Scope clause (\"focus on commits unique to this branch vs `<base>`\") — *PR #1018*.\n3. **Commits in scope (this design):** the rendered briefing block + read instructions.\n4. Per-run prompt textarea, if user provided one — *PR #1018*.\n\nThe user's per-run prompt stays last so its directive feels freshest to the agent.\n\n### Rendered brief — example\n\n```\nCommits in scope (newest first):\n\n  commit     checkpoint     files  agent        subject\n  9411f0c4   a3b2c4d5e6f7   1      claude-code  Drop now-unused nolint:ireturn on LauncherFor\n  78f1134f5  —              0      —            Merge remote-tracking branch 'origin/main' into feat/...\n  92c2e6d2   —              1      —            Extract masterBaseBranch constant to unblock CI goconst\n  47eb589ad  6b2243c45a68   3      claude-code  Address PR review feedback: data-loss, cleanup, context, ...\n\nFor each commit with a checkpoint ID, run `entire explain <id>` to see the\nauthor's stated intent, friction, and open items (run\n`entire explain <id> --raw-transcript` for the full transcript). For commits\nwithout a checkpoint ID, use `git show <commit>` directly. Gr…7078 chars truncated…oint has implementation session plus review session | Agent column uses the latest non-review implementation session, not `agent_review` |\n| Checkpoint has only review sessions | Agent column renders `—` |\n| Trailer present but checkpoint unresolvable | Row shows `<id> (unresolved)` |\n| Long subjects | Truncated to ~80 chars |\n| Newest-first ordering | Snapshot-stable, matches `git rev-list` default |\n| Only `origin/main` exists, no local `main` | Uses the resolvable remote ref for `rev-list`, while displaying `main` |\n\nEach renderer test builds a temp repo via `testutil.InitRepo`, commits with controlled trailer presence/absence, optionally writes checkpoint metadata via the existing `WriteCommitted` helper, and calls `renderReviewBriefForRepo(ctx, tmpDir, scope)` directly. Those tests can use `t.Parallel()` because they do not change process CWD. Any command-level test that must use `t.Chdir()` remains explicitly non-parallel. Per CLAUDE.md, use `testutil.InitRepo` not direct `git.PlainInit`.\n\n### `composeReviewPrompt` integration tests\n\n- `TestComposeReviewPrompt_IncludesBrief` — non-empty brief lands between scope clause and per-run prompt (locks in section ordering).\n- `TestComposeReviewPrompt_OmitsEmptyBrief` — empty brief produces no orphan header or extra blank lines.\n- Existing tests get a small parameter update; `composeReviewPrompt(skills, \"\")` preserves prior behavior.\n- `TestBuildReviewPrompt_ThreadsBrief` — verifies the `runReview` prompt-building helper includes a rendered brief without needing to launch a real agent.\n\n### `runReview` / multi-agent orchestrator tests\n\n- Brief computation failure → review still launches with a brief-less but complete prompt.\n- For multi-agent: brief is computed **once** at the orchestrator level and shared across all per-agent compose calls.\n\n### Integration test (`cmd/entire/cli/integration_test/`)\n\nNo prompt-capture integration test is required for the PR #993 single-agent path: `entire review --track-only` intentionally returns before prompt composition, and launching a real agent is out of scope. If PR #1018's headless/Vogon path exposes a deterministic captured prompt, add one integration/E2E canary there instead: temp repo, two commits with checkpoints + one without, run the canary agent, assert the prompt contains the expected commit rows.\n\n### Vogon canary (`e2e/vogon/`, `cmd/entire/cli/agent/vogon/`)\n\nAdd one regex assertion to a Vogon E2E test: the prompt Vogon receives contains the \"Commits in scope\" header line. Catches prompt-assembly regressions in `test:ci` without API cost.\n\n**Per CLAUDE.md:** if the prompt template changes, Vogon's regex parsers may need updating. Run `mise run test:e2e:canary` after any change to `composeReviewPrompt`.\n\n### Out of scope for testing\n\n- Real-agent E2E. Per CLAUDE.md: \"Do NOT run E2E tests proactively.\" Real-agent verification belongs to manual smoke testing during implementation review.\n- What configured review skills *do* with the brief — that's the skill's contract, not ours.\n- Renderer performance on large branches — bounded by `git rev-list` which is fast; revisit if a real perf concern surfaces.\n\n## Rejected approaches\n\n### Approach 1 — Pointer-only prompt; agent runs `entire context list` via Bash\n\nOriginal Section 2 of brainstorming. Telling the agent \"run `entire context list` for scope\" forces a Bash round-trip just to discover what's being reviewed. The list is *information*, not a tool — information belongs in the prompt. Rejected for the on-launch pre-rendering above.\n\n### Approach 2 — New `entire context list` standalone command\n\nConsidered as an internal-use command shared between `entire review` and ad-hoc user invocation. Rejected because:\n\n- Once the brief is pre-injected into the prompt, the agent never needs to call the command — there is no caller.\n- \"Users might want it ad-hoc\" is speculative; nobody asked for it.\n- A CLI surface is a maintenance commitment (docs, JSON output stability, version-skew). An internal helper has none.\n\nIf a real user request appears later, lifting the helper into a Cobra command is ~30 lines. v1 stays internal.\n\n### Approach 3 — Materialized review pack on disk\n\nConsidered: write `.entire/review/<run-id>/` before launching, with copies of every in-scope checkpoint's session dirs, and tell the agent to read files from there. Rejected because:\n\n- Disk I/O cost grows with branch size; the brief-in-prompt approach pays only the cost the agent chooses to incur.\n- Cleanup adds state to manage; the brief-in-prompt approach has no on-disk artifact.\n- Pre-copying decisions still pre-curate (which sessions to include, what filenames). The brief approach delegates *all* read decisions to the agent via `entire explain`.\n\n### Approach 4 — Pre-render full transcripts / summaries into the prompt\n\nConsidered: embed every checkpoint's `Summary` (and possibly transcripts) into the prompt at launch. Rejected because:\n\n- Token-cost cliff on long branches.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":7375},"formatted_output":"","status":"completed"}}
